  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4a/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_25.png",
  "plot_summary": "Reed urges Ben to drink a formula he's accidentally stumbled upon that will permanently change him back to Ben Grimm, but the Thing smashes it because Alicia likes him as the Thing.&nbsp; He only wants a formula that will allow him to change back and forth!&nbsp; Alicia then arrives with a newspaper that announces that the Avengers have returned to the US after replacing the Hulk with Captain America.&nbsp; But what happened to the Hulk?\n\nAt that moment, out in New Mexico, the Hulk catches a ride on a truck (after demanding its drivers to \"Let me ride in your truck, and I'll let you live!\").&nbsp; But as they drive away, he suddenly reverts back to \"Bob\" Banner and hops out at the next stop, \"heading for the hills!\"&nbsp; (He makes it to his underground lab.)\n\nNot far away, the Avengers are examining some of the wreckage left behind by the Hulk's takeover of the truck.&nbsp; Captain America has now joined their ranks, and they've traveled out to New Mexico in search of the Hulk.&nbsp; But the trail runs cold, and they're forced to return to New York City.\n\nMeanwhile, \"Bob\" Banner has changed back to the Hulk, and finding himself surrounded by the scientific machinery owned by \"Bob\" Banner, he decides to smash it to prevent himself from ever becoming the puny human Banner ever again.&nbsp; He even decides to empty his pockets, but finds a newspaper clipping that details how the Avengers have replaced the Hulk in their ranks with the newly-revived Captain America.\n\nThe Hulk is outraged, and he decides to head back to New York City to \"destroy the Avengers <i>forever</i>!\"&nbsp; Even though his legs make \"powerful, distance-swallowing leaps,\" it still takes him 24 hours to finally reach the East Coast.\n\nBy the time the Hulk arrives, the scene shifts back to the Baxter Building, where Reed has suddenly taken ill, so Johnny takes the Fantasti-Car to go fetch the doctor, but he spots the Hulk below, smashing up a building.&nbsp; He takes off as the Torch to investigate, but is bested by the Hulk, who leaves him since he's actually looking for the Avengers.\n\nThe Hulk's rampage causes the police commissioner to summon the FF for help, but Ben says that Reed is sick.&nbsp; Johnny is gone, but Reed, who can't go himself, urges Ben and Sue to go after the Hulk, warning Ben that the Hulk is far stronger -- he must use his wits!&nbsp; They get to the Hulk just as Johnny recovers, and Sue tries to protect him from the Hulk with her force shield, but the strain is too much, and she loses consciousness.\n\nThe Thing finally confronts the Hulk face-to-face.&nbsp; Though they've met before (in 1962's [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12]), the Hulk is not interested in the Thing.&nbsp; He wants only the Avengers and intends to smash his way through anything that gets in his way!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=And so it is that \"the two titans confront each other... heralding the start of one of the greatest hand-to-hand battles in recorded history!\"]\n\nAnd indeed, the two combatants go at each other for page after page after page... while a helpless Reed watches the action on television, knowing that he could help, but completely unable to do anything.&nbsp; The folks on <i>Yancy Street</i> are paying attention, and they even try to <i>help</i> the Thing, saying, \"We're not lettin' anyone <i>else</i> pulverize our favorite whippin' boy!\"\n\nBut as the fight progresses, the Thing notes that he's growing tired while it seems the Hulk continues to grow ever-stronger!&nbsp; Meanwhile, back at the Baxter Building, Sue has finally found the doctor, who had warned Reed against working so much with the \"strange microbes\" and \"untested formulae\" (that Reed exposed him to while working on a formula to help Ben).&nbsp; As a result, Reed has caught a virus and needs complete rest.&nbsp; Sue says \"Oh, Reed... my darling... I... never realized how much I love you... <i>need</i> you!\"\n\nBack at the battle, the Hulk finally bests Ben and turns his attention back to the city, as it's the <i>Avengers</i> he really wants!\n[BR] In his eyes, the Thing has been nothing but a minor hindrance.\n\nHowever, the now-abandoned Thing pulls himself up, astonished that he's been beaten, but he'll have none of it!&nbsp; Maybe he needed the pounding to make him realize he's not unbeatable.\n\n\"Well, like my dear ol' Aunt Petunia used to say...'You can only die once!'\n[BR] And that's the <i>only</i> way he'll stop me now -- by <i>killin'</i> me!!\"\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "A wildly contrived showdown delivers exactly what readers paid money to see.\nBut they walk away with a memorable lesson of what makes Ben Grimm so special.",
  "subtitle": "The Day Ben Grimm Lost the Battle... But Won Our Hearts",
  "review_text": "It wasn't the <i>first</i> Thing/Hulk battle (<i>that</i> happened back in [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12]), but it was certainly the most <i>extensive</i> one to date!&nbsp; And one look at that cover showing the Hulk absolutely <i>towering</i> over the Thing suggests that the fate of Ben Grimm seems very much in doubt!&nbsp; The so-called \"<b>BATTLE OF THE CENTURY!</b>\" was an excellent way to attract readers, so kudos to Jack Kirby and company.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=I was hooked... even <i>before</i> I opened the magazine!]\n\n[ASSET: left | AvengersInTheNews | width=25%] To early Marvel readers, every sign has pointed to something like this happening.&nbsp; The Avengers essentially battled the Hulk over the course of their first three issues (which I wrote about in my [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01] review).\n[BR]\n[BR] You just have to admire the way Stan and Jack <i>immediately</i> tied the events of <i>this</i> issue to what had just happened over in <i>that</i> title just one publication week earlier (which is, of course, the dramatic return of the genuine Captain America in [LINK:1964:AVEN_4|AVEN #4|2027-04-25]).\n[BR]\n[BR] If the cover hadn't been indication enough, the story seems determined to follow up on that ongoing thread, except now in the pages of <i>Fantastic Four</i>!\n\nTo that I say:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Sign me up!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Amazing that I'm already salivating, and I'm still only on the top of page 3!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | NoFormulaForMe | width=19%] What's even more amazing is that, even <i>before</i> Sue reads aloud the newspaper for the benefit of the blind Alicia, the first couple of pages featured a wary Thing smashing Reed's latest formula aimed at permanently transforming him back to the human Ben Grimm, <i>despite</i> the fact that it would mean the loss of \"one fourth\" of the team's fighting strength.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even amidst the build-up leading to this \"Battle of the Century,\" Stan and Jack don't forget to deliver characterization to the title team members -- [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=well done!]\n\nWhat Reed seems to have forgotten, however, is the promise he made to his friend on page 10 of last April's [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16], where Ben spells it out for him very plainly --\"...forget about tryin' to change me back...\"&nbsp; Ben asks him there to devote his energies instead to trying to help Alicia see again -- which, to Reed's credit, he at least <i>tries</i> to do in [LINK:1963:FF_19|FF #19|2026-12-04]).\n\nWhile it's true that Reed sees the truth -- that Alicia loves <i>Ben Grimm</i>, and not just the rocky Thing (as Ben supposes), it's far likelier that <b>Stan and Jack</b> are the ones who have forgotten this prior conversation from nine months earlier -- even if readers (or someone like me) haven't, <i>heh</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I mean, <i>come on</i>!&nbsp; Stan can't even remember Banner's first <i>name</i>, much less what happened in this magazine back in April!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Did I mention that I'm still only on <b>page 3</b>?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Already, I'm rubbing my hands together in anticipation!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | LetYouLive | width=20%] Of course, not everything was sunshine and roses, as that same page features the Hulk hijacking a truck out in New Mexico, demanding a lift, and even threatening its drivers with the line [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"Let me ride in your truck and I'll let you live!\"]&nbsp; <b><i>Geez!</i></b>\n[BR]\n[BR] I know the Hulk is constantly being hunted, and he certainly has no love of \"puny humans,\" but I don't recall him ever threatening to <i>kill</i> a couple in this manner, [SPAN: severity=error; text=so this strikes me as wildly out of character.]\n[BR]\n[BR] Furthermore, Marvel has already established that the Hulk can cover vast distances simply by making giant leaps, so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=why did he need a lift in the <i>first</i> place?]&nbsp; This is <i>literally</i> shown shortly afterward, where on page 5, he states that his leg muscles \"push me hard enough to leap for <i>miles</i>!\"\n\nAlong the way, Stan reminds everyone how he's forgotten the name of Hulk's alter-ego (perhaps writing this issue right around the same time as last month's [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01]).&nbsp; The Hulk's random transformation back to \"Bob\" Banner serves as a refresher for readers out there who might not have been familiar with the character.\n\nAs shocking as it seems that the writer can't even seem to get the character's name right, putting myself in the context of someone trying to understand the publishing environment of the day, I can <i>kind of</i> see what Stan and Jack are trying to do, <i>especially</i> given that it's been an entire <i>year</i> since the Hulk had his own magazine.&nbsp; And even though he's appeared in <i>Avengers</i> more recently, [SPAN: severity=info; text=none of those adventures were <i>ever intended</i> to focus solely on the Hulk.] In that sense, the scene functions almost like a miniature Hulk origin recap hidden inside a Fantastic Four story.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: As for the \"Bob\" snafu, which started in <i>Avengers</i> #3, Stan's mistake was pointed out to him in an upcoming letters page, and he was forced to retroactively give Dr. Banner the full name of \"Robert Bruce Banner.\" Thus, he saved face in the end, but I'm <i>sure</i> that it was actually just a mistake, <i>heh</i>.]\n\nBut speaking of mistakes, page 5 has the Avengers out in New Mexico, surveying the damage the Hulk caused to the hijacked truck.\n\nThere, we find Captain America saying that, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Only the <i>Hulk</i> could have done this!\"]\n[ASSET: center | AvengersAppear | width=large]\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=How on Earth would <i>he</i> know?&nbsp; He's never even <i>met</i> the Hulk!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(something he's very quick to point out to Rick Jones on page 10 of this month's Avengers issue)]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And <i>curse</i> you, Jack Kirby, for once again showing Iron Man flying with his knees bent!&nbsp; <b><i>Arrgh!</i></b>)]\n\nAgain... I <i>get it</i> -- it's a story, and the intent is to build up to something, particularly in light of what seems to be coming next month.&nbsp; I suppose it is important to show the Avengers out engaging in their favorite pastime -- getting in a little [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Hulk hunting] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(copyright pending)].&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=(Still...)]\n\nFrom a storytelling perspective, what is largely going unnoticed is how the Hulk and his history with the Avengers quietly consumes all of pages 4-6 (and the majority of page 3 as well).&nbsp; I must say that having the Hulk become outraged that the Avengers have chosen to replace him with \"someone named Captain America\" was clever, as it gives the Hulk an actual motive to return to NYC.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=If only he realized this isn't an issue of <b><i>Avengers</i></b> but rather <b><i>Fantastic Four!</i></b>]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(heh...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThere was just one more thing for Stan and Jack to do... which was to sideline the other members of the Fantastic Four, all to force the Thing into solo action against the arriving Hulk, who is now in the middle of a rampage through New York!\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Everyone, run for your lives!]\n\n[ASSET: left | ReedIsIll | width=12%] [ASSET: right | HesJustAKid | width=12%] And this is <i>exactly</i> what Stan and Jack proceed to do over the course of 4+ pages,\n[BR]\n[BR] * first taking Reed out of the equation by having him be sick (page 7),\n[BR] * knocking out the Human Torch simply because he is no match for the Hulk (page 8),\n[BR] * and finally forcing the Invisible Girl to expend her force field energy by trying to protect her brother against the Hulk (page 11)\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=That leaves just the Thing... <i>exactly</i> as the co-creators planned...]\n\nSay what you want about the absolutely unabashed and [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=BLATANT PLOT DEVICE] that is [SPAN: severity=error; text=big enough to drive a truck through], it nevertheless deposits readers squarely at the midway point of the story, all of them [SPAN: severity=success; text=clamoring] for the main event, this [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"<b>BATTLE OF THE CENTURY!</b>\"]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=From this point forward, only the Thing stands between the Hulk and the populace!]\n[ASSET: center | ButterUpYourPopcorn | width=large]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Readers, <i>this</i> is where you pause to go butter up that popcorn!&nbsp; This baby is about to get <i>lively</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhat follows was nothing less than a [SPAN: severity=success; text=joy to read.]&nbsp; Not only did Jack Kirby provide battle sequences that were top-notch (as always), Stan's dialogue was pitch-perfect from essentially their first blows, with the Thing quipping, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"I'll <i>remember</i> those words, playmate!&nbsp; They'll look great carved on your headstone!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=PERFECT!]\n\n[ASSET: left | IEatPizzaWithThatHand | width=13%] What's extra refreshing about seeing the Thing take on an opponent who is <i>obviously</i> out of his league is the way he never loses his sense of humor.\n[BR]\n[BR] Imagining an extended fight scene between the Hulk and, say, Thor (who could actually give him a run for his money), you would expect the Thunder God to be dismissive of the Hulk, as if he were just one more nuisance to be waved off with his almighty hammer.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: And the all-too-brief battle between the two that we saw in <i>Avengers</i> 3 would get an extended replay later this year in the pages of <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> 112.]\n\n[HANG0] Ben Grimm, however, is different.&nbsp; He seems to understand that the Hulk is more powerful -- more brutal -- right from the start... [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=but he's simply too stubborn to care!]\n\nTo him, the equation is quite simple:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=The Hulk must be stopped, and there's no one else there who can do it!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HeGoingAboutItWrong | width=24%] [ASSET: right | MeanwhileOnYancyStreet | width=31%] The opening sequence lasts for more than 3 pages before Jack pulls back momentarily to remind us that the name of the magazine isn't [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=The Thing], but rather <i>Fantastic Four</i>.\n[BR]\n[BR] But though Reed seems to understand that Ben is relying solely on brawn to take down the Hulk (a strategy he says is \"...all wrong!\"), he isn't in any condition to provide leadership.\n[BR]\n[BR] And elsewhere, we even get to see members of the Yancy Street Gang trying to help out, and you just have to laugh at their sentiment which essentially is, \"If anyone's going to torture the Thing, it's going to be <i>us!</i>\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=These are great character beats!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HowDoesHeDoIt | width=12%] These brief interludes buy Jack Kirby another three pages to focus entirely on the Thing versus Hulk.&nbsp; The two eventually find themselves on a suspension bridge (identified as the \"Washington Bridge\" on page 20).&nbsp; There, despite all his bravado, you finally see the first signs of genuine concern on the Thing's face.\n[BR]\n[BR] While <i>he's</i> slowly tiring, the Hulk shows no signs of slowing down!\n[BR] Indeed, we all join the Thing in wondering, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"How does he <i>do</i> it?\"]\n\n[ASSET: right | SueNeedsReed | width=21%] Once again though, the story returns to Reed (this time with Sue and the doctor) long enough to provide yet <i>another</i> excellent moment, where Reed's serious illness causes Sue to realize how much he means to her (page 20).\n[BR]\n[BR] Readers who were around for [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1] might remember that she identified herself as Reed's fiance.&nbsp; But they might <i>also</i> remember how Stan and Jack have been a bit more coy with Sue's feelings ever since, <i>especially</i> in the light of the Sub-Mariner's arrival.&nbsp; When Reed has wanted to discuss their relationship and the \"understanding\" they seem to have had, Sue has been hesitant more recently to commit.\n\nBut this short scene seems to settle the matter once and for all, and inserting it in the middle of an issue that otherwise sidelines both of them, [SPAN: severity=success; text=I thought was brilliant.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs the page count mounted, it was time to bring the Thing/Hulk battle to a close, and to no one's surprise, the Hulk comes out on top.&nbsp; To him, the Thing has been nothing more than an annoyance, and after finally putting him down, he angrily turns back to hunt for the elusive Avengers.\n\nThat leads to the issue's dramatic cliffhanger showing Ben dragging himself up onto his feet, and far from being satisfied that he'd done all that he was capable of doing, he steadfastly readies himself for another bout.\n\nAfter all, as his Aunt Petunia was fond of saying:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=\"You can only die once!\"]\n[ASSET: center | YouCanOnlyDieOnce | width=large]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Killing him is exactly what the Hulk is going to have to do!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nStill... <i>um</i>... I think it's fair to ask:\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Whatever happened to the Fantasti-Car that Johnny abandoned on page 7?]\n[HANG] Is it still hovering up there above the city?\n\nAs for me, I have a couple of guesses:\n[HANG] 1) We're supposed to assume it was programmed to return to the Baxter Building on auto-pilot, <b>OR</b>...\n[HANG] 2) Readers were never supposed to ask this question!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(My money is on the <i>latter</i>!&nbsp; What <i>other</i> explanation is there?)]\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nYou have to wonder about the plotting session between Stan and Jack.&nbsp; Was it much more than \"Hey Jack, let's have the Thing and Hulk battle for most of the issue.&nbsp; Have them fight for a really long time!&nbsp; Make sure to end it with the Thing pulling himself off the ground, determined to finish the fight!&nbsp; I'll make up the rest!\"\n\nThis left Jack free to fill up half a story with little other than all-out-action, and... <i>well</i>... [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=he's really good at that!]\n\nEven though we've seen hints of Ben's sarcastic side before, I think this is the first time we've truly seen what makes him special, which is his pig-headed stubbornness, his unwillingness to quit, even when the odds are not in his favor.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=That's what this issue spotlights.]\n\nThe cover showing the Hulk towering over the Thing strongly hints that Ben is out of his league, and as Reed points out, that is very likely true, but even though the outcome seems foreshadowed right from the start, one thing is also absolutely certain:\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=Ben Grimm is someone readers can root for!]\n[BR] (And no prior issue of the <i>Fantastic Four</i> has demonstrated it quite the way this one does.)\n\nWere there problems with the plot?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text= Yes!]\n[BR] Were the rest of the Fantastic Four essentially shunted off to the side?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Absolutely!]\n[HANG] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Need I remind you that Johnny has been absent since page 11?)]\n\nBut more importantly:&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Do we care?]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=small; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Probably not!]\n\nDespite the plot's shortcomings, the sight of a defeated Thing dragging himself off the ground and doggedly turning to reengage an enemy he seems to have [SPAN: severity=error; text=no prayer of defeating] is more than worth the price of admission!\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=Kudos to Stan and Jack for a memorable issue!]\n\nDespite the fact that Stan has forgotten the Hulk's name, despite the convenience of Reed's illness, the ease with which the Hulk dispatched the Human Torch (which essentially took the Invisible Girl out of the fight as well) -- <b>despite all these plot contrivances </b>that do, in the end, result in a loss of points in the story's final rating, readers can still point to this issue and [SPAN: severity=success; text=tip their caps for one well worth the read!]\n\nYou've probably already been able to figure out that I'm not one who usually is caught up in the spectacle of the latest \"<b>BATTLE OF THE CENTURY</b>,\" but this issue was different.&nbsp; It <i>transcended</i> being merely the spectacle of two heavyweights in all-out combat, and honestly, I wonder just how many readers were left out there who really wanted to see another battle involving the Hulk (as the <i>Avengers</i> had been laden with all too many, of late).\n\nYet here, it wasn't the <i>spectacle</i> that carried the tale.\n[BR] When all was said and done, [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=characterization won the day] (particularly in the case of Ben).\n\nEven in the midst of a battle that was all-too staged, the co-creators delivered both:&nbsp; <b>spectacle</b> <i>and</i> <b>character</b>.\n[BR] And, in doing so, they didn't ignore the rest of the cast either ([SPAN: fontsize=small; text=except maybe Johnny]).\n\nDespite all its flaws, [SPAN: severity=success; text=this issue succeeds where it matters most.]&nbsp; Long after readers have forgotten which warehouse was smashed, which bridge was damaged, or how Reed conveniently wound up bedridden at the very moment his team needed his leadership, they'll remember one thing above all others:\n\n[SPAN: display=block; textalign=center; severity=info; fontsize=extralarge; fonttype=bold; text=Ben Grimm got back up!]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * The splash page indicates that this tale is \"double-length.\"&nbsp; Curious, since it seems to be about the same length as any other full-length magazine, so perhaps they meant that the tale was so long, it had to spill over into the next issue.\n\n[HANG0] * Page 5 indicates that the Avengers have arrived in New Mexico in a helicopter.&nbsp; While it's not <i>explicitly</i> stated that they travelled all the way from New York City in the helicopter, the story seems to <i>imply</i> that, but that does lead me to scratch my head.&nbsp; The 1964 record of 1,348 miles was set later that year, but <i>most</i> helicopters at the time had a maximum range of 200-400 miles.&nbsp; (But then again... they didn't have someone named <b>Tony Stark</b> aboard, <i>heh</i>!)\n\n[HANG0] * The Thing/Hulk battle claimed at least one building, a bus, a Jeep, someone's speed boat, and a good bit of one NYC's suspension bridges, not to mention part of one abandoned building and another abandoned warehouse (lucky all these \"abandoned\" structures seem to litter the streets of NYC!).&nbsp; And... <i>well...</i> what about all the damage out in <i>New Mexico</i>?"
  ],
  "letters_page": "[DEVNOTE: shellhead]\n\nMarc Bailey refers to Iron Man (while discussing <i>Tales of Suspense</i>) as \"Shell Head\" -- in quotes.&nbsp; The response from Marvel asks for \"more respect for our heroes\" and that they don't call him \"shell-head.\"&nbsp; But remember that the Hulk already called Iron Man by that name in [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08], so it's unlikely that Marc Bailey actually coined the name.\n\nRichard O'Baid's letter cracked me up when he pointed out that his doctor, making a house call, was distracted by a copy of [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17] lying nearby while taking his temperature and indicated \"I'm going to tell my son to start buying it so I can read it too.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=Amazing!]",
  "highlight": "The Fantastic Four are intentionally sidelined to pave the way for the first extended Thing versus Hulk battle, but even in defeat, it's Ben character that wins the day."
}